   596	#### 4.2.1 Go version literals and canonical comparison
   597	
   598	The `go` entry is complete, so its classifiers are exhaustive here and are not
   599	deferred to a driver decision.
   600	
   601	Go does **not** use one version grammar in the two directive positions, so
   602	Curator does not either. Each position is pinned to the upstream artifact that
   603	governs it, with no widening anywhere. Writing `INT` for `(0|[1-9][0-9]*)`:
   604	
   605	| Grammar | Position | Definition |
   606	|---|---|---|
   607	| `goModVersionLiteral` | the whole value of the `go` directive | `^([1-9][0-9]*)\.(0\|[1-9][0-9]*)(\.(0\|[1-9][0-9]*))?([a-z]+[0-9]+)?$` |
   608	| `goToolchainNameShape` | the whole value of the `toolchain` directive | `^default$\|^go1($\|\.)` |
   609	| `goToolchainVersion` | the version part of a toolchain name, after the upstream split | `^INT(\.INT(\.INT\|[a-z]+(INT)?)?)?$` |
   610	
   611	`goModVersionLiteral` is `golang.org/x/mod/modfile.GoVersionRE` verbatim: a
   612	nonzero major, a **required** minor, an optional patch, and an optional
   613	prerelease suffix, each component free of leading zeros.
   614	`goToolchainNameShape` is `modfile.ToolchainRE` verbatim.
   615	`goToolchainVersion` is the language accepted by `cmd/go/internal/gover.Parse`,
   616	which is what upstream applies to the version part of a toolchain name: a bare
   617	major is admitted (`gover.Parse("1")` is `1.0.0`), and a prerelease suffix after
   618	an explicit patch is not, because upstream rejects `1.21.3rc1` on the stated
   619	grounds that it would order opposite to `1.21 < 1.21rc1`.
   620	
   621	A value that matches its position's grammar yields a **base triple** — absent
   622	minor and patch components are `0` — and a prerelease flag set by the trailing
   623	`[a-z]+` group. Comparison against the resolved toolchain uses the base triple
   624	only, under the canonical order of section 2.1.
   625	
   626	Using the base triple is exact rather than approximate, which matters because
   627	Go's own order is `1.21 < 1.21rc1 < 1.21.0`: a bare language version sorts
   628	*below* its own release candidates. Both canonicalizations — language version
   629	`1.21` to `(1, 21, 0)` and prerelease `1.21rc1` to `(1, 21, 0)` — can differ
   630	from Go's order only for a comparand strictly between `1.21` and `1.21.0`, and
   631	every such value is itself a prerelease. The resolved toolchain is never a
   632	prerelease, because Stage A step 5 rejected one before Stage B ran. The two
   633	orders therefore agree on every comparison Stage B can actually perform.
   634	
   635	**Curator's grammars are not a superset of Go's at any position, deliberately.**
   636	An earlier draft used one wider literal grammar for both directives and argued
   637	that the extra values were harmless because they are assertions rather than
   638	selectors. That is true of *selection* and false of *ordering*: a wider grammar
   639	would let a value Go itself rejects — `go 1`, `go 0`, `go 1.023` — pass Stage B
   640	as a permitted comparison and reach cache lookup and a compiler child, where it
   641	fails as a compiler error after the manager has already committed to the build.
